# Git workflow

## Branches

| Branch | Role |
|---|---|
| `staging` | Integration / implementation branch — all feature work lands here |
| `main` | Production-ready — only updated by merging `staging` + bumping version |

- Feature work happens directly on `staging` (no per-task branches needed) unless the user asks otherwise.
- Production deploys are triggered by **published** GitHub Releases, not by pushing to `main`.

## Commit messages (Conventional Commits)

Subject ≤72 chars, imperative mood, **scoped**. **Task commits must link a work item** in square brackets — see `07-jira-commit-linking.mdc`.

```
feat(<area>)[DO-123]: <summary>
fix(<area>)[DO-123]: <summary>
chore(<area>)[DO-123]: <summary>
refactor(<area>)[P2-01]: <summary>   # roadmap-only when no Jira mirror
docs(<area>)[DO-123]: <summary>
test(<area>)[DO-123]: <summary>
ci(<area>)[DO-123]: <summary>
build(<area>)[DO-123]: <summary>
perf(<area>)[DO-123]: <summary>
```

Use `[DO-N]` when the task is on Jira; `[P*-*]` only when no Jira issue exists. Do **not** use Smart Commit commands (`#time`, `#comment`, `#resolve`) — Jira status/worklog via MCP only.

**Allowed scopes**: `repo`, `ci`, `web`, `backend`, `worker`, `data`, `utils`, `types`, `config`, `prisma`, `auth`, `incidents`, `dispatch`, `vehicles`, `personnel`, `stations`, `skills`, `alliance`, `notifications`, `i18n`, `infra`, `plan`, `memory`.

Plan-file verifier commits: `chore(plan)[DO-123]: mark P8-07 verified` / `chore(plan)[P8-07]: mark P8-07 verified` (prefer `[DO-N]` when mirrored).

Session memory (`.cursor/skills/agent-memory/`) is **gitignored** — local working notes only. Verifiers post outcomes to Jira; do not commit session files.

## Staging rules

- **Never** `git add .` or `git add -A`. Always stage explicit paths.
- One task = **one implementation commit** (or plan-file verifier commit when applicable). No session memory commits.
- Never amend commits that have been pushed.

## Push policy

**Never push** unless the user explicitly asks. Local commits only.
This includes never running `git push --force`, `git push --tags`, or any push command in scripts or hooks.

## Examples

```bash
# GOOD
git add apps/backend/src/modules/auth/auth.service.ts \
        apps/backend/src/modules/auth/auth.service.spec.ts
git commit -m "feat(auth)[P2-01]: add register endpoint with bcrypt cost 12"

# BAD — blanket staging
git add .

# BAD — no scope / no task id
git commit -m "added auth"
```
